---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "Terms of Use | ShouldISpend";
const description = "The terms for using ShouldISpend calculators, guides, and educational financial planning tools.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <style>
    .policy-page {
      width: 92%;
      max-width: 920px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 0 96px;
    }

    .policy-page .eyebrow {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1.8px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .policy-page h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4rem);
      line-height: 1;
      letter-spacing: -0.045em;
      margin: 0 0 28px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page h2 {
      font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      margin: 44px 0 14px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page p,
    .policy-page li {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.04rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
    }

    .policy-page strong {
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page a {
      color: var(--primary);
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .policy-page {
        padding: 54px 0 76px;
      }

      .policy-page h1 {
        font-size: 2.45rem;
      }
    }

    .trust-closeout-section {
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding-top: 28px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .trust-closeout-section h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .trust-closeout-section p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

  </style>

  <div class="policy-page">
  <main class="page-shell">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Site terms</p>
      <h1>Terms</h1>
      <p>
        By using ShouldISpend, you agree to use the site as an educational planning tool. The calculators and guides
        are designed to help you think through financial pressure, not to make decisions for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-card">
      <p><strong>Last reviewed: July 2026.</strong> These terms are part of the site trust and policy layer for ShouldISpend.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-card">
      <h2>Educational Use Only</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend provides calculators, examples, and general information for educational purposes. The site does
        not provide personalized financial, legal, tax, investment, medical, insurance, or professional advice.
      </p>

      <h2>No Guarantee of Results</h2>
      <p>
        Calculator results depend on the numbers and assumptions entered by the user. A result may not reflect every
        real-world factor, including local costs, taxes, interest rates, insurance rules, job stability, family needs,
        medical needs, or emergencies.
      </p>

      <h2>User Responsibility</h2>
      <p>
        You are responsible for checking your own numbers, reviewing your financial situation, and consulting qualified
        professionals when a decision has major financial, legal, medical, tax, or investment consequences.
      </p>

      <h2>Acceptable Use</h2>
      <p>
        You may use ShouldISpend for personal planning and research. Do not misuse the site, interfere with its operation,
        scrape it abusively, attempt unauthorized access, or use the content in a misleading way.
      </p>

      <h2>Content Changes</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend may update, revise, remove, merge, noindex, or reorganize pages at any time to improve accuracy,
        quality, usefulness, or site performance.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitation of Liability</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend is provided as-is. To the fullest extent permitted by law, ShouldISpend is not responsible for
        losses, damages, or decisions made based on use of the site.
      </p>

      <h2>Related Policies</h2>
      <p>
        Please also review the <a href="/about/">About</a> page, <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>, <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>,
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>, and <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about these terms can be sent through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  </div>

      <section id="TrustTermsCloseout" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Using the site</h2>
        <p>
          Should I Spend is provided for general educational and planning purposes. By
          using the site, readers understand that calculators are estimates based on the
          inputs provided and that the final decision remains the reader's
          responsibility. The site is not a substitute for professional advice or for
          checking actual quotes, contracts, bills, policies, and due dates.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers should not rely on the site for emergencies, legal deadlines, medical
          decisions, tax filings, credit decisions, or any situation where a qualified
          professional or provider must review the facts.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
        <p>
          Readers may use the calculators for personal planning and comparison. The site
          should not be used to submit malicious code, disrupt service, scrape at
          abusive volume, copy the site in a way that misrepresents ownership, or send
          sensitive personal information through contact channels.
        </p>
        <p>
          Calculator outputs may change as pages are improved, assumptions are clarified,
          and examples are updated. A past result should be rechecked before a real
          purchase, lease, bill payment, application, booking, or other commitment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Content and responsibility</h2>
        <p>
          The written explanations, calculator framing, examples, and page structure are
          provided to help readers think through spending decisions. Readers are
          responsible for verifying facts that apply to their situation, including
          prices, fees, interest rates, insurance requirements, local rules, contract
          terms, and provider policies.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustTermsCloseoutChanges" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Changes to calculators and pages</h2>
        <p>
          Should I Spend may revise calculators, examples, assumptions, page structure,
          explanations, and links as the site is improved. Readers should refresh and
          recheck a page before relying on an older result for a current spending
          decision, especially when prices, fees, rates, or policies may have changed.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustTermsCloseoutNoGuarantee" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>No guarantee of outcome</h2>
        <p>
          Using the site does not guarantee that a reader will save money, avoid debt,
          receive approval, qualify for a lease, obtain a lower price, or make the best
          possible decision. Calculator results are based on the inputs provided and the
          assumptions shown on the page. Different facts can produce a different result.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers should use the site as one planning step alongside real quotes,
          written agreements, provider policies, professional advice when needed, and
          their own budget records. Continued use of the site means the reader accepts
          those limits.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustTermsCloseoutFinalResponsibility" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Final responsibility for decisions</h2>
        <p>
          Readers are responsible for deciding whether information from the site fits
          their own situation. A calculator can help organize a decision, but it cannot
          know every contract term, local rule, provider requirement, employer policy,
          account balance, debt obligation, family need, or timing issue that may affect
          the final choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before making a purchase, signing a lease, booking travel, choosing a payment
          plan, accepting a loan, or committing to any meaningful expense, readers should
          compare the calculator result with current quotes, written terms, actual due
          dates, and their own budget records. If the decision has legal, tax, medical,
          insurance, credit, housing, or long-term financial consequences, the reader
          should seek qualified professional guidance.
        </p>
      </section>

      </Layout>